    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/PSU_Academic/job/College-of-Medicine/Postdoctoral-Scholar---Pediatric-Oncology-Translational-Research-Lab_REQ_0000082769": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar - Pediatric Oncology Translational Research Lab",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants . This is a term position; length of the term will be discussed during the interview process. Continuation past the term length discussed will be based on university need, performance, and/or availability of funding.   POSITION SPECIFICS The Beat Childhood Cancer Research Consortium at Penn State College of Medicine, Department of Pediatrics, in Hershey, PA, is seeking an innovative and highly driven Postdoctoral Scholar for the Pediatric Oncology Translational Research Lab (POTRL). This is a full-time on-site position. The Beat Childhood Cancer Research Consortium (BCC) is an international clinical research sponsor and acts as a Contract Research Organization (CRO) of a network of 50+ hospitals that participate in BCC's investigator-initiated clinical trials. The Pediatric Oncology Translational Research Lab is the preclinical research team of BCC. This advanced-level postdoctoral position aims at developing cutting-edge research on pediatric oncology with focus on pre-clinical and translational aspects of cancer. Job Summary Independent research Design, execute, and troubleshoot experiments with minimal faculty oversight, applying expert-level judgment to resolve technical challenges. Formulate original hypotheses grounded in the current pediatric oncology literature and develop testable experimental frameworks. Maintain rigorous, reproducible laboratory records and uphold data integrity standards across all projects. Manage personal project timelines and deliverables, proactively communicating progress and roadblocks to the lead researcher and PI. High-impact scholarship Generate publication-ready datasets and lead the writing, revision, and submission of first-author manuscripts to high-impact journals. Critically interpret complex, preclinical, or clinical translational datasets and communicate findings to diverse audiences. Present research at national and international conferences; contribute to preparation of abstracts and poster materials. Serve as a peer reviewer for relevant scientific journals when invited. Technology development Identify, evaluate, and implement cutting-edge methodologies (e.g., single-cell sequencing, spatial transcriptomics, CRISPR screens, PDX/organoid models) relevant to pediatric cancer biology. Establish and validate new protocols, optimizing them for the lab's specific research questions and infrastructure. Document new methods thoroughly and train lab personnel on their implementation. Team collaboration Actively participate in lab meetings, journal clubs, and departmental seminars; contribute constructive scientific feedback to peers. Foster productive relationships with clinical collaborators, research technicians, bioinformaticians, biostatisticians, and translational teams within and outside the institution. Collaborate across multi-PI consortium projects, coordinating experimental contributions and adhering to shared timelines. Co-author multi-investigator publications and contribute equitably to collaborative grant applications. Mentorship & training Directly mentor rotation students and graduate students in experimental techniques, scientific reasoning, and professional conduct. Provide regular, constructive feedback on mentees' data, presentations, and written work. Assist in development of laboratory training materials, SOPs, and onboarding resources. Model a culture of rigor, intellectual curiosity, and psychological safety within the team. Lab stewardship & funding Ensure compliance with institutional biosafety, IACUC, and IRB protocols; complete all required training on time. Contribute substantially to the writing and revision of grant applications (R01, K99/R00, foundation awards) as a co-investigator or named personnel. Identify and independently apply for independent fellowships and career development awards to support the transition to faculty. This is a limited-term position funded for one year from date of hire, with possibility of refunding.  The following clearances must be successfully completed for this position: Criminal Background Check   Minimum Experience: PhD 8+ years of research experience Relevant publications in the field Experience with in vitro (organoids), and in vivo (PDX) cancer models Demonstrated capacity for grant application Preferred areas of interest include, but are not limited to: Pharmacology, Molecular Biology, Pathology, Immunology The Pennsylvania State University is committed to and accountable for advancing diversity, equity, and inclusion in all of its forms. We embrace individual uniqueness, foster a culture of inclusive excellence that supports both broad and specific diversity initiatives, leverage the educational and institutional benefits of diversity, and engage all individuals to help them thrive. We value inclusive excellence as a core strength and an essential element of our public service mission.     BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES Employment with the University will require successful completion of background check(s) in accordance with University policies.       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional well-being.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
    },
    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/PSU_Academic/job/College-of-Medicine/Postdoctoral-Scholar---Comparative-Medicine_REQ_0000082476-2": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar - Comparative Medicine",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants . This is a term position; length of the term will be discussed during the interview process. Continuation past the term length discussed will be based on university need, performance, and/or availability of funding.   POSITION SPECIFICS The Penn State College of Medicine in Hershey, PA in Comparative Medicine is seeking a Postdoctoral Scholar. This position is a postdoctoral training position that is designed for graduate veterinarians (DVM, VMD, or equivalent) seeking advanced training in laboratory animal medicine and comparative biomedical research. The fellow will rotate through diverse research environments and gain hands‑on experience providing veterinary care for commonly used laboratory animal species, including mice, rats, rabbits, livestock species, and zebrafish. Primary Responsibilities Provide clinical veterinary care, health monitoring, and preventive medicine for laboratory animal species Support biomedical research by advising investigators on animal model use and welfare considerations Train research staff and students in proper animal handling and techniques Participate in Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC) activities, including protocol review, inspections, and post‑approval monitoring Complete a mentored research project with the goal of presentation and first-author publication Participate in educational activities such as seminars, rounds, and didactic coursework Required Qualifications DVM, VMD, or equivalent veterinary degree from an accredited institution For graduates of non-accredited foreign veterinary schools, ECFVG (Educational Commission for Foreign Veterinary Graduates) certification is required A valid, unrestricted veterinary license in at least one U.S. state  Demonstrated interest in laboratory animal medicine, comparative medicine, or biomedical research Strong written and verbal communication skills Ability to work collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment Who We Are The Comparative Medicine Animal Resource Program supports Penn State’s research mission through excellence in animal care, regulatory oversight, and scientific collaboration. Our Laboratory Animal Medicine Training Program is recognized by the American College of Laboratory Animal Medicine and prepares trainees for professional careers through comprehensive clinical, regulatory, and scholarly training. Learn more about the program and the didactic curriculum at: https://med.psu.edu/residencies-fellowships/professional-programs/laboratory-animal-medicine-training-program   BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES This position requires the following clearances in addition to applicable background checks: PA State Police Criminal Background Check, PA Child Abuse History Clearance Form, and Federal (FBI) Fingerprint Criminal Background Check.       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional well-being.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
    },
    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/PSU_Academic/job/College-of-Medicine/Postdoctoral-Scholar---Medicine_REQ_0000082825-3": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar - Medicine",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants . This is a term position; length of the term will be discussed during the interview process. Continuation past the term length discussed will be based on university need, performance, and/or availability of funding.   POSITION SPECIFICS The Penn State College of Medicine, Department of Medicine in Hershey, PA is seeking a Postdoctoral Scholar to join our newly established program, The Sleep-Vascular Program at Penn State led by Dr. Rami Khayat. The team works on understanding the pathways and treatment options of hypertension and endothelial dysfunction in obstructive sleep apnea (OSA). Ongoing projects range from molecular studies of the effect of intermittent hypoxia on nitric oxide biology in human endothelial tissue to human studies using cardiovascular physiology measurements to understand the pathways of hypertension and its treatment options in patients with OSA. Newly NIH funded projects also include mathematical modeling and machine learning of models of tissue hypoxia using sleep studies and molecular markers. The team uses quantitative RNA measurements, immunohistochemistry and confocal imaging techniques, and is developing measurements using HPLC, enzymatic assays, and chemiluminescence. In-vivo human studies utilize  skin biopsy techniques, endothelial reactivity, echocardiography, microneurography measurements of sympathetic nerve activity, and microdialysis.  The new postdoctoral scholar will lead a translational project that evaluates the effect of intermittent hypoxia on sympathetic and endothelial functions in humans and tissue models. Cardiovascular studies will be conducted by a dedicated team of vascular physiologists. The scholar will participate in the cardiovascular physiology studies and lead ex-vivo and in-vitro experiments in human tissue and cultured endothelial cells using intermittent hypoxia exposure.  The scholar will collaborate and receive support from a senior project manager who oversees the wet-lab operations. The project requires interaction with patient-volunteers and knowledge of clinical research regulation. Minimum Requirements: MD or PhD (completed by start of employment) in biological sciences discipline or equivalent. A technical background is required regardless of the Ph.D. degree;  Experience with laboratory techniques including transcription regulation, imaging, tissue-on-chip, vascular biology, oxidative biology, or hypoxia research Some experience with human research or completion of required training Experience with statistical software Proven writing abilities through publications Excellent verbal communication skills. Desired Qualifications: Experience in multi-disciplinary collaborative research Knowledge of analysis software and machine learning techniques   BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES Employment with the University will require successful completion of background check(s) in accordance with University policies.       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional well-being.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
    },
    "https://psu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/PSU_Academic/job/College-of-Medicine/Researcher_REQ_0000082563-3": {
      "title": "Researcher",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nAPPLICATION INSTRUCTIONS: CURRENT PENN STATE EMPLOYEE (faculty, staff, technical service, or student), please login to Workday to complete the internal application process . Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. CURRENT PENN STATE STUDENT (not employed previously at the university) and seeking employment with Penn State, please login to Workday to complete the student application process. Please do not apply here, apply internally through Workday. If you are NOT a current employee or student, please click “Apply” and complete the application process for external applicants .   Approval of remote and hybrid work is not guaranteed regardless of work location. For additional information on remote work at Penn State, see  Notice to Out of State Applicants . This is a term position; length of the term will be discussed during the interview process. Continuation past the term length discussed will be based on university need, performance, and/or availability of funding.   POSITION SPECIFICS Researcher Key Responsibilities for Researcher position Scientific Leadership and Project Management Lead and coordinate basic and translational research projects focused on urinary tract infection, mucosal and epithelial biology, host–pathogen interactions, aging, and related areas. Take responsibility for advancing assigned projects from experimental planning through data generation, analysis, interpretation, and publication. Coordinate research activities involving multiple investigators, trainees, research staff, core facilities, and collaborators. Monitor project progress, identify scientific or operational barriers, and develop strategies to maintain momentum and achieve research objectives. Evaluate emerging findings and relevant scientific literature to identify new hypotheses, experimental approaches, and opportunities for collaboration. Experimental Research and Scientific Oversight Design, conduct, oversee, and troubleshoot complex experiments using molecular, cellular, microbiological, imaging, and in vivo approaches. Provide expertise in experimental (mouse) models of urinary tract infection and associated epithelial, immune, and inflammatory responses. Support studies utilizing primary tissues, cell culture, organoid systems, genetically modified mouse models, and other translational disease models. Develop, optimize, and standardize experimental protocols and workflows. Ensure rigor, reproducibility, appropriate controls, and quality assurance across experimental studies. Perform specialized experiments when advanced scientific or technical expertise is required. Analysis of human samples for UTI studies Scientific Support Across the Research Group Serve as a senior scientific resource for faculty investigators and research teams. Assist faculty with development and execution of new or ongoing research projects. Support collaborative and multi-investigator studies within and outside the research group. Team Development Train personnel in specialized experimental methods and promote rigorous and reproducible research practices. Participate actively in laboratory and project meetings, journal clubs, and scientific discussions. Help foster an intellectually engaged, collaborative, and productive research environment. Present research findings at laboratory meetings and national or international scientific conferences, contribute to abstracts, presentations, progress reports, and other scientific communications. Maintain high standards for scientific documentation, data integrity, and reproducibility. Required Qualifications PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in microbiology, immunology, molecular biology, cell biology, biomedical sciences, or a closely related discipline. Postdoctoral research training and experience required. Demonstrated record of scientific productivity, including peer-reviewed publications. Demonstrated ability to independently design, execute, troubleshoot, analyze, and interpret complex research studies. Experience leading or coordinating scientific projects and advancing projects toward publication. Strong expertise in molecular, cellular, microbiological, and/or in vivo experimental approaches. Experience working collaboratively with faculty investigators, trainees, technical staff, and scientific collaborators. Excellent scientific writing, data interpretation, organizational, and communication skills. Ability to manage multiple concurrent scientific priorities and provide leadership within a collaborative research environment. Preferred Qualifications Research experience in urinary tract infection, bacterial pathogenesis, mucosal or epithelial biology, host–pathogen interactions, aging, or related areas . Experience with mouse models of infection, inflammation, aging, or related disease processes. Experience with genetically modified mouse models and experimental cohort planning. Experience with primary tissues, advanced imaging, organoid systems, or other translational experimental models. Demonstrated experience mentoring students, research technologists, postdoctoral fellows, or other research personnel. Strong publication record with evidence of substantial intellectual contributions to research projects. Experience contributing to NIH or other federally funded research programs, including manuscripts, preliminary data, progress reports, and grant applications.   BACKGROUND CHECKS/CLEARANCES Employment with the University will require successful completion of background check(s) in accordance with University policies.       BENEFITS Penn State provides a competitive benefits package for full-time employees designed to support both personal and professional wellbeing. In addition to comprehensive medical, dental, and vision coverage, employees enjoy robust retirement plans. One of the standout benefits is the generous 75% tuition discount, available to employees as well as eligible spouses and children.   For more detailed information, please visit our  Benefits Page. (Note: For Postdoctoral benefits, please see our Postdoctoral Benefits page.)   CAMPUS SECURITY CRIME STATISTICS Pursuant to the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act and the Pennsylvania Act of 1988, Penn State publishes a combined Annual Security and Annual Fire Safety Report (ASR). The ASR includes crime statistics and institutional policies concerning campus security, such as those concerning alcohol and drug use, crime prevention, the reporting of crimes, sexual assault, and other matters. The ASR is available for review here .   EEO IS THE LAW Penn State is an equal opportunity employer and is committed to providing employment opportunities to all qualified applicants without regard to race, color, religion, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability or protected veteran status. If you are unable to use our online application process due to an impairment or disability, please contact 814-865-1473.   Penn State is committed to and accountable for advancing equity, respect, and belonging. We embrace individual uniqueness, as well as a culture of belonging that supports equity initiatives, leverages the educational and institutional benefits of inclusion in society, and provides opportunities for engagement intended to help all members of the community thrive. We value belonging as a core strength and an essential element of the university’s teaching, research, and service mission.   Federal Contractors Labor Law Poster PA State Labor Law Poster Penn State Policies Copyright Information Hotlines"
